WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.910
This is the gala guest list tool my team of four built for a New York nonprofit.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.350
I led the backend.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.030
Here's one gala, start to finish, on invented data.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.210
Mei, the gala admin, signs in with a six-digit code from her email.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:21.530
There are no passwords, and only people on the staff list get in.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:25.140
Ticket sales come in from Give Lively.

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:28.920
Each ticket type is linked to a sponsorship package once.

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:33.240
Then one import adds twenty-seven new orders and twenty-one guest names,

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:38.160
and close calls, like a possible duplicate or a refund, wait for a person.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.110
Acme's check came in the mail.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:44.730
Daniel records it: twelve thousand five hundred dollars,

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.090
the check number, and the day it arrived.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:50.210
The app never takes card or bank details.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:55.190
Jordan, at Acme, gets a reminder email.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.830
Its button opens Acme's private sponsor portal, with no account needed,

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.890
where sponsors name their guests until the deadline.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:09.310
Staff can also invite guests directly, like Sam, a guest of the Executive Director.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.870
Each QR ticket is made only when the invitation sends,

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:17.850
the database keeps only its hash, and it works until the morning after the gala.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:21.970
On gala night, Sam holds up the ticket at the door.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.130
The iPad's camera reads it and says who came in.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.330
At the top, the iPad shows it's synced, live.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:30.850
Then the Wi-Fi drops.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.590
Kenji searches for Riley Chen and taps check in anyway, with no connection at all.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:40.190
The check-in waits on the iPad, and when the Wi-Fi is back, it sends itself.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.490
If two iPads check in the same guest, the first arrival wins.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.690
Back on the dashboard, arrivals count up live from the door.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.560
My part was the backend: who can see and change what,

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.580
the private links and tickets, and the sync behind the door.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:00.200
Every person and record here is invented.
